3. EXPECTED ABSENCE VERSUS UNEXPECTED ABSENCE

The information content of absence depends upon expectation.

Let:

E(X)=P(X\ expected).

Then absence becomes increasingly informative as expectation rises.

Conceptually:

Information(\neg X)
\uparrow
\quad\text{as}\quad
P(X)\uparrow.

This produces a powerful Pantheon rule:

\boxed{
Do not merely detect what is missing.
Measure how surprising its absence is.
}

A missing chair in an empty field means little.

A missing chair at a table set for twelve means more.

An empty chair with eleven people staring at it becomes narrative machinery.

Same absence.

Different relational field.

